# ==============================================================================
# CODEOWNERS — who must review what
#
# Docs: docs/CI_CD.md · docs/GITHUB_WORKFLOW.md
#
# This file is what turns "please be careful with these files" into a rule that
# GitHub enforces. A pull request that touches a path listed here cannot be
# merged until a code owner approves it — which is what makes the "two approvals
# for supabase/migrations and lib/github" rule real rather than aspirational.
#
# HOW TO READ THIS FILE
#   * The LAST matching pattern wins.
#   * `*` matches everything and must come first.
#   * Owners are individual @usernames, listed in the role table below.
#
# WHY INDIVIDUALS AND NOT TEAMS
#   A CODEOWNERS entry that references a team which does not exist matches
#   nothing. Combined with "require code owner review" in branch protection,
#   that means a pull request can NEVER be merged — the requirement is simply
#   unsatisfiable. Individual usernames always resolve, so this file works the
#   moment it is committed.
#
#   Once the club creates @Open-Source-Software-Club/core-team,
#   @Open-Source-Software-Club/technical-leads and
#   @Open-Source-Software-Club/design-team in the organisation, replace the
#   username groups below with the team names. The comments mark exactly which
#   lines change; nothing else about the file needs to move.
#
# ROLE TABLE (from README.md § Maintainers & Core Team)
#   core-team        — every maintainer: President, both Vice Presidents, both
#                      Technical Leads, Secretary, Joint Secretary
#   technical-leads  — @NizzyStark (Siddharth C), @Yashwanth-202011 (Yashwanth Gowda H)
#   design-team      — no dedicated design lead is listed yet; core-team covers
#                      design paths until one is appointed
#
# REVIEW REQUIREMENTS (enforced by .github/branch-protection/ruleset.json)
#   1 approval        — everything
#   2 approvals       — lib/github, supabase/migrations, lib/rbac
#                       (a second approval is required because these paths hold
#                       tokens, trust boundaries and authorisation decisions)
# ==============================================================================

